Relevant columns and how they will be used  
• form_log.session : identify each individual visit sequence (the key we finally count)  
• form_log.path  : pick only the two target pages (/regist/input → /regist/confirm)  
• form_log.stamp : sort the two page‑views in time so that “input” comes before “confirm”  
(Optionally)  
• form_log.action : could be limited to 'view' in case other actions exist  

Typical filter conditions  
path = '/regist/input'          -- first step  
path = '/regist/confirm'         -- second step  
action = 'view'             -- if you only want page‑views  
stamp ordering : stamp_input < stamp_confirm  

Below are 10 exploratory → final SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows where it makes sense).  
Enclose identifiers in double quotes if your environment requires it.

```sql
-- 1. See every different path recorded in form_log
SELECT DISTINCT path
FROM form_log
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at all registration‑related rows
SELECT *
FROM form_log
WHERE path LIKE '/regist/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Sessions that hit the /regist/input page at least once
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM form_log
WHERE path = '/regist/input'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Sessions that reached the /regist/confirm page at least once
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM form_log
WHERE path = '/regist/confirm'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Sessions that saw either of the two pages
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM form_log
WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. For each session, record first timestamps of each page
SELECT
  session,
  MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) AS first_input,
  MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm
FROM form_log
WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
GROUP BY session
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Keep only sessions that have both pages *and* input comes before confirm
SELECT session
FROM (
  SELECT
    session,
    MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) AS first_input,
    MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
  GROUP BY session
)
WHERE first_input  IS NOT NULL
  AND first_confirm IS NOT NULL
  AND first_input   < first_confirm
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. FINAL ANSWER: number of unique sessions that followed the required order
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT session) AS num_sessions
FROM (
  SELECT
    session,
    MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) AS first_input,
    MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
  GROUP BY session
  HAVING first_input IS NOT NULL
     AND first_confirm IS NOT NULL
     AND first_input < first_confirm
);
```

```sql
-- 9. Same result using a self‑join instead of conditional aggregation
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT i.session) AS num_sessions
FROM   form_log AS i
JOIN   form_log AS c
       ON  i.session = c.session
       AND i.stamp   < c.stamp
WHERE  i.path = '/regist/input'
  AND  c.path = '/regist/confirm';
```

```sql
-- 10. Another variant exploiting an EXISTS test
SELECT COUNT(*) AS num_sessions
FROM (
  SELECT DISTINCT session
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path = '/regist/input'
) AS t
WHERE EXISTS (
  SELECT 1
  FROM form_log f
  WHERE f.session = t.session
    AND f.path    = '/regist/confirm'
    AND f.stamp   > (SELECT MIN(stamp)
                     FROM form_log
                     WHERE session = t.session
                       AND path = '/regist/input')
);
```

